# What GitHub counts as this repository's languages.
#
# Linguist works by file extension, so left alone it reports this as part JavaScript, part
# Python, and — since the benchmark grew a C, a Java and a Go file — part all three of
# those as well. None of that is what anybody wrote here. `bench/` holds the same loop in
# six languages so the README's numbers can be re-derived by anyone, and the editor support
# is JavaScript because that is what VS Code takes. Marking them keeps them in the
# repository and out of the summary.
bench/**     linguist-vendored
editors/**   linguist-vendored

# `.lr` is Luarust's own extension, and Linguist has never heard of it, so the eleven files
# that are actually written *in the language this repository is for* count as nothing at
# all. That stays true until Linguist learns the name, which takes the language being used
# somewhere other than here. Naming them as Lua or Rust would make the bar look right by
# saying something false about what they are, so they go on counting as nothing.
*.lr         text
